# golden-session.jsonl -- the CROSS-LANGUAGE golden fixture for the context-quality
# definitions that bin/context_quality.py and core/src/telemetry/session-metrics.ts
# MIRROR: effective_input, the scoreable-assistant predicate, the model->window map,
# and the ">window => 1M" inference net.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FILE. Two tests read THIS file and golden-session.expected.json:
#   the python golden-session suite        (tests/ in the ctxmonitor package)
#   core/tests/session-metrics.test.ts     ("golden fixture (cross-language)", brokkr)
# They assert the SAME numbers from the SAME two files. So a definition changed in ONE
# language now BREAKS THAT LANGUAGE'S assertion against the shared expectation -- the
# mirror stops being a comment that nothing enforces, which is how these two definitions
# were previously kept in step (badly: drift was found by archaeology, not by a suite).
# NEVER repair a failure by editing the expected file until it matches whatever the code
# prints today. Decide which language is RIGHT, fix BOTH, then restate the expectation.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE DOES NOT COVER. The two implementations do NOT agree on non-canonical
# token values (a negative, a numeric string, a boolean): python's `int(x or 0)` accepts
# them, the TS toTokenInt clamps them to 0. Such a record cannot live here at all -- the two
# languages would compute different sums, so no SHARED expectation could exist for it. That
# split is real and pre-existing; it is recorded, with both answers and a per-language
# assertion, under `known_divergences` in golden-session.expected.json. Read that before
# concluding this fixture proves the two definitions agree everywhere: it does not.
#
# Synthetic content only -- no real transcript material. ASCII only, no NUL bytes.
# The '#' lines are not JSON, so context_quality.py's fail-open _parse_jsonl_lines skips
# them; the TS test's loader mirrors that fail-open skip (the one place the two loaders
# part company is recorded under known_divergences too).
# RECORD INDICES in the expected file are therefore indices into the PARSED record list,
# not raw line numbers -- and each record's uuid ends in its own index (...-0000000000NN)
# so the expected file's scoreable_uuids cross-check the indices independently.
#
# WHAT EACH RECORD IS FOR (index: why it exists)
#   1,3    plain scoreable turns, 1M-tier model (cache-creation turn, then cache-read turn)
#   4,5    a sidechain assistant + its sidechain user record -- subagent turns are EXCLUDED
#   6      a '<synthetic>' error placeholder WITH usage -- synthetic-ness is the only reason
#          it is excluded, and its 77000 tokens would move every sum if that check broke
#   7      an assistant with NO usage key at all              -- usage-absent exclusion
#   8      an assistant with usage: null                      -- usage-null exclusion
#   9      null input_tokens / null cache_creation            -- the `int(x or 0)` discipline
#   10     input_tokens / cache_creation MISSING entirely     -- same discipline, absent form
#   12     a LEGACY 200k model (haiku) as the final turn of view `prefix_legacy_final`,
#          after only 1M-tier turns -- pins window-from-the-FINAL-model on a mixed session
#   14     a second sidechain, with large usage (a broken sidechain check moves the sums)
#   16     a legacy 200k model carrying 250000 effective input -- over its detected window,
#          so view `prefix_over_window` TRIPS the ">window => 1M" inference net
#   18     usage PRESENT but all zero -- a scoreable turn that contributes nothing
#          (distinct from 7/8, where usage is absent: those are not turns at all)
#   19     isSidechain present and FALSE -- must still be scoreable
#   21     the FINAL scoreable turn: claude-opus-4-10-... -- the digit-boundary case. The
#          200k token "opus-4-1" is a substring of "opus-4-10"; only the (?!\d) boundary
#          keeps this 1M model out of the 200k tier. Its effective input (187500) is
#          deliberately BELOW 200000, so a broken boundary is NOT rescued by the inference
#          net and shows up as window 200000 / occupancy 0.9375 instead of 1M / 0.1875.
#   23,24  a synthetic and a sidechain record AFTER the last real turn -- pin that the
#          final-turn selection skips them (else the final model/window/occupancy move)
#   0,2,11,13,17,20,22,25  user records -- never scoreable
